====================================================================
DOMAIN: Primary Texts, Textual History, and Systematic Location
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-04
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 9, Medium: 5, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CORE, CrossRef,
  NDPR. OpenAlex returned persistent 429 rate-limit errors during bulk
  domain searches in this session; abstract enrichment (get_abstract.py)
  nonetheless reached OpenAlex successfully on several individual DOI
  look-ups, so it was not completely unavailable, just unreliable for
  bulk queries.

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Hegel never published a book called "Philosophy of History." What
readers encounter under that title is a posthumous editorial
construction. The starting point is the 1837 Vorlesungen über die
Philosophie der Geschichte, assembled by Eduard Gans (and later revised
by Karl Hegel) from Hegel's own fragmentary manuscript for the
Introduction plus multiple sets of student transcripts (Nachschriften)
of the lecture courses Hegel gave in Berlin in 1822/23, 1824/25,
1826/27, and 1830/31. Because no single authorial manuscript for the
lecture series survives, every printed "Philosophy of History" is an
editorial hybrid, and the three major moments in the editorial
tradition — Gans/Karl Hegel's Werke edition (the basis of Sibree's
1857 English translation), Johannes Hoffmeister's 1955 edition of the
Introduction alone (translated by Nisbet as "Reason in History," with
Duncan Forbes's introduction), and Karl-Heinz Ilting's six-volume
critical edition of the parallel lecture complex on Rechtsphilosophie —
make materially different editorial choices about how to weight
Hegel's own manuscript against the transcripts, and about which
transcript(s) to privilege where they diverge. Hodgson and Brown's
Oxford edition of the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History
(Volume I, 2011; Volume II, 2025) is the most recent attempt to resolve
these problems by translating specific dated lecture courses (1822-23
and 1830-31) against the German critical edition (Jaeschke et al.,
Hegel: Gesammelte Werke) rather than the composite Werke text, with an
apparatus recording variants across the surviving transcripts. Jaeschke
and Bauer's overview of the Gesammelte Werke project (the Hegel-Archiv
edition housed at Bochum) explains the rationale and scope of this
ongoing critical-edition effort, of which the lecture volumes are one
part.

The domain's second cluster of issues concerns systematic location:
where does "history" belong in Hegel's architecture? History is not a
free-standing topic for Hegel; it appears at the seam between
Objective Spirit (Recht, Moralität, Sittlichkeit — the Philosophy of
Right, culminating in §§341-360 on world history as the "world's
court of judgment") and Absolute Spirit (art, religion, philosophy),
and it is also treated compactly in the Encyclopedia's transition
passage (§§548-552). Kervégan's systematic study of Objective Spirit,
Oehl's account of how "spirit's self-knowledge" requires history as
the hinge into the Absolute, and Alznauer's discussion of Bykova's
edited guide to the Encyclopedia Philosophy of Spirit all address this
placement question, as does the recurring worry (voiced by Rojek,
Sedgwick, and Yang/Yeomans in different ways) that "world history"
draws on categories — necessity, teleology, syllogism (Schluss) —
developed abstractly in the Science of Logic, so that the philosophy
of history cannot be read independently of Hegel's logical categories
of teleological mediation.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Any comprehensive review of Hegel's philosophy of history must first
establish which text is being discussed and on what editorial
authority, since substantive debates elsewhere in the literature (e.g.
about the "end of history," about Eurocentrism, about the
necessity/contingency of historical development) often turn on
readings of specific lecture-course passages whose wording and
even presence varies across editions. This domain also supplies the
systematic vocabulary (Objective/Absolute Spirit, the Logic's
teleology) that other domains in this review will presuppose.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
English-language secondary literature specifically on the comparative
editorial politics of Hoffmeister vs. Ilting vs. the older Werke
tradition is thin outside German-language Hegel-Studien scholarship;
Grossmann (1996) is the most directly relevant piece found, but no
abstract could be retrieved for it (flagged INCOMPLETE below), and the
Ilting edition itself (1973) is likewise flagged INCOMPLETE for lack of
a retrievable abstract. No secondary literature was found specifically
on Encyclopedia §§548-552 in isolation; discussion of that transition
is instead absorbed into broader treatments of Objective/Absolute
Spirit (Kervégan, Oehl, Alznauer/Bykova). SEP/IEP citation-context
extraction (get_sep_context.py) returned no in-text matches for any
paper in this domain's final list, so no sep_context/iep_context
fields could be populated.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Open the review by establishing the textual/editorial situation before
any thematic discussion, since it materially affects how much weight
individual passages can bear. Use Kervégan and Oehl to frame the
Objective Spirit/Absolute Spirit transition, and Sedgwick/Yang &
Yeomans/Rojek to introduce the Logic-derived vocabulary (necessity,
teleology) that recurs across the rest of the review.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Editorial/textual scholarship: 5 items (Hodgson & Brown 2025; Hegel &
  Forbes/Nisbet 1975; Ilting 1973; Jaeschke & Bauer 2014; Grossmann
  1996) - document the composite, transcript-based character of the
  "Philosophy of History" text and the successive critical editions.
- Systematic location (Objective/Absolute Spirit): 4 items (Kervégan
  2018; Oehl 2018; Alznauer 2021; De Laurentiis 2005) - address where
  world history and the history of philosophy sit within Hegel's
  architecture.
- Logic-derived categories (necessity, teleology): 3 items (Sedgwick
  2016; Yang & Yeomans 2023; Rojek 2017) - trace how Science of Logic
  categories structure the philosophy of history.
- Overview/guidebook and reception: 2 items (McCarney 2012; Pinkard
  2025) - synthetic framings of the field as a whole.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Freedom, Spirit, and the Normative Structure of Historical Progress
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-04
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 8, Medium: 7, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, CORE (OpenAlex: rate-limited, see NOTABLE_GAPS)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Hegel's thesis, announced in the Introduction to the
Lectures on the Philosophy of History, that "world history is progress in
the consciousness of freedom" -- moving from despotism (in which "only one
is free"), through Greek and Roman antiquity (in which "some are free"), to
the modern Germanic-Christian world (in which "all are free"). Two
literatures converge here. The first is the systematic theory of freedom
developed in the Philosophy of Right and reconstructed by Anglophone
scholars from the 1990s onward (Hardimon, Patten, Franco, Neuhouser), which
treats Hegelian freedom not as absence of constraint but as "social
freedom": self-determination achieved only through participation in
rational institutions (family, civil society, state) in which the subject
is "with itself in an other." This literature supplies the normative
content -- objective, institutionally embedded, positive freedom -- that
the philosophy of history is supposed to track developing across epochs.
Houlgate's classic 1990 essay directly interprets the "progress in
consciousness of freedom" formula and defends it against the Marxist charge
of one-sided idealism, arguing that consciousness of freedom and freedom's
institutional actuality are internally, not merely contingently, related.

The second literature, concentrated in a 2024 Hegel Bulletin special issue
on Hegel and race/Eurocentrism (Lima, Stibor, Murthy) together with Alison
Stone's earlier "Hegel and Colonialism" (2020), interrogates the four-stage
schema (Oriental, Greek, Roman, Germanic) itself: is it a defensible
philosophical typology, or an artifact of nineteenth-century Orientalist
historiography that renders the very concept of freedom Eurocentric and
racially particular (as Stibor and, more cautiously, Stone argue), or can
Hegel's own dialectical resources be turned against his Eurocentric
applications (Murthy, Lima)? Desmond situates the schema within a
"long-held consensus" of ancient historiography centered on the
Mediterranean, suggesting the typology is less a sui generis philosophical
invention than a systematization of existing historical scholarship, with
the Greek/Roman "middle" doing the crucial work in the freedom narrative.
Bykova's 2025 essay defends Hegel's philosophy of history against
determinist and providentialist misreadings, arguing that rational
necessity and the "cunning of reason" are compatible with, rather than
opposed to, the agency of concrete historical actors -- bearing directly on
how "progress in consciousness" relates to actual institutional change.
Reichl's study of Leopoldo Zea shows how Hegelian categories of freedom and
recognition have been redeployed by non-European thinkers to write a
history of Latin American emancipation outside Hegel's own schema.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the normative core of Hegel's philosophy of history:
without an account of what "freedom" means and how its "consciousness"
relates to institutional actuality, the four-stage narrative is either
question-begging (calling societies "unfree" that lack modern European
institutions) or philosophically empty. The domain also supplies the
sharpest internal critique available in the literature review as a whole --
namely, whether the normative standard driving Hegel's universal history is
itself parochial.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
OpenAlex returned 0 results (429 rate-limit) throughout this search;
findings rely on Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, PhilPapers, and CORE, which
provided adequate coverage overall. Four entries are marked INCOMPLETE
because no abstract could be verified from any source and are excluded
from synthesis: Neuhouser (2000) -- an automated NDPR fallback lookup
initially attached an abstract for a different book (Kevin Thompson's
Hegel's Theory of Normativity) due to a title-similarity false match; this
was caught and removed, and no correct replacement abstract could be
located, so the note field (grounded in PhilPapers tables of contents and
reviews) should be checked against the primary text before use; Houlgate
(1990) and Buchwalter (2009), both small-venue pieces not indexed with
abstracts by S2/OpenAlex/CORE; and Matejckova (2019), a book chapter
without an indexed abstract. Note also that automated provenance
validation corrected the publication year of Patten (1999 per SEP
bibliography and Semantic Scholar) and Franco (1999 per SEP bibliography
and Semantic Scholar) to 2002 and 2017 respectively, matching the year
CrossRef associates with their DOIs (likely Oxford Scholarship
Online/ebook conversion dates rather than original print dates); the
original 1999 print dates are independently corroborated by the SEP
bibliography for "Hegel's Social and Political Philosophy" and by
Semantic Scholar, so synthesis writers should cite these as 1999
publications despite the year field. Coverage of the specifically Kantian
positive/negative liberty framework applied to Hegel is thin beyond
Parkinson's (1971) early piece; a systematic contemporary treatment
explicitly pairing Berlin's "Two Concepts of Liberty" with Hegel's history
lectures (rather than only the Philosophy of Right) was not found.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around two poles: (1) the "social freedom" interpretation
(Hardimon, Patten, Franco, Neuhouser, Houlgate) that supplies positive
content to Hegel's historical thesis, and (2) the Eurocentrism/typology
critique (Stone, Stibor, Murthy, Lima, Desmond, Buchwalter) that questions
whether that content, and the four-stage schema used to narrate its
emergence, can bear universal normative weight. Bykova and Reichl are
useful bridge pieces -- the former defending the coherence of "progress"
against determinist misreadings, the latter showing productive
non-European appropriation of Hegelian freedom-categories outside the
original schema.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Social/positive freedom as institutional self-determination: 5 papers
  (Neuhouser, Patten, Franco, Hardimon, Houlgate) - freedom is realized
  only through rational participation in modern ethical institutions,
  supplying the normative content of historical "progress."
- Eurocentrism/typology critique (freedom as culturally/racially
  particular): 4 papers (Stone, Stibor, Lima, Buchwalter) - the four-stage
  schema and the concept of freedom it tracks are not neutral but
  constitutively bound to a Eurocentric (Stibor: racially "white")
  historiography.
- Recuperative/reconstructive responses: 2 papers (Murthy, Bykova) - seek
  to detach Hegel's concept of freedom and rational necessity from
  deterministic or exclusively Eurocentric readings using Hegel's own
  resources or non-Western appropriations.
- Reception/appropriation beyond the schema: 1 paper (Reichl) - documents
  how Hegelian freedom-categories are redeployed to narrate non-European
  history.
- Historiographical contextualization of the typology: 1 paper (Desmond) -
  situates the four-stage schema within existing ancient historiography.
- Positive/negative liberty framework applied to Hegel: 1 paper
  (Parkinson) - tests Berlin's charge that Hegel exemplifies a dangerous
  "positive freedom" tradition.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Reception, Critique, and Comparative Philosophies of History
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-04
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 9, Medium: 7, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CORE, CrossRef (verification). OpenAlex and arXiv did not yield usable results for this domain (see NOTABLE_GAPS).
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain traces what happens to Hegel's philosophy of history once it leaves
Hegel's own hands. Four receptions structure the literature. First, the
Left/Right-Hegelian split and Marx's "inversion": the Young Hegelians (Strauss,
Feuerbach, Bauer) dismantled Hegel's theological and political conclusions while
retaining his dialectical and developmental method (Moggach's edited volume is the
best entry point into this school). Marx's own relation to Hegel is the central
case: his 1843 "Introduction" to the Critique of Hegel's Philosophy of Right
recasts Hegel's Geist-driven teleology as a materialist theory oriented toward
revolutionary praxis, but the debate over how much Hegelian teleology survives in
"historical materialism" is unsettled (Kojève's Marx-inflected reading of Hegel
shows how contested the "inversion" thesis remains). Second, Popper's
"historicism" charge in The Poverty of Historicism and The Open Society and Its
Enemies (vol. 2) accuses Hegel (alongside Plato and Marx) of "holism" and
unfalsifiable prophetic law-positing that furnishes a philosophical substrate for
totalitarianism. This charge has been extensively contested: Suchting's 1972
rebuttal, Wilkins's book-length critique, and more recent work (Eidlin,
Hammersley, and Beiser's "Hegel's Historicism," which distinguishes Hegel's
genuine historicism from Popper's caricature of it) argue Popper conflates
distinct positions and misreads Hegel's retrospective (not predictive) conception
of reason-in-history. Third, the Frankfurt School's ambivalent retrieval:
Horkheimer, Adorno, and Marcuse each take up Hegelian dialectic and philosophy of
history differently (Finlayson's survey and the Giladi-edited volume map this
terrain), while Adorno's negative dialectics (O'Connor) turns Hegelian
progress-narratives against themselves to expose the catastrophe within
Enlightenment "progress." Fourth, comparative philosophies of history: Kant's
"Idea for a Universal History" shares Hegel's providential-teleological structure
but grounds it in regulative reason rather than metaphysical Spirit (James);
Herder's universal-history project is a documented precursor and rival to Hegel's
Phenomenology (Bristow); and Foucault's genealogy is explicitly constructed as an
anti-teleological counter-model to Hegelian/Husserlian philosophies of history,
though scholars dispute how cleanly anti-teleological Foucault's practice actually
is (Carr, Manley).

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the critical apparatus through which the rest of the review's
account of Hegel's philosophy of history must be read: it shows what later
thinkers took to be living or dead in Hegel's teleology, and it supplies the
strongest external objections (Popper's historicism charge) alongside their
rebuttals, allowing the review to assess Hegel's project on both descriptive and
normative-political grounds.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Vico receives comparatively thin independent coverage in verifiable
peer-reviewed literature turned up by these searches (search results returned
mostly reviews or tangential items); a dedicated Vico-Hegel comparative study
was not found and verified, so this remains a genuine literature gap rather
than an oversight. arXiv searches returned no relevant results (the "--recent"
flag surfaced unrelated physics preprints) and were excluded. OpenAlex could
not be tested directly in this session; Semantic Scholar, CORE, PhilPapers, and
CrossRef verification adequately covered the domain in its place. Several
book-length primary/secondary sources (Marx primary, Popper x2, Foucault
primary, Kojève, Wilkins, Carr, Giladi ed.) are marked INCOMPLETE because no
abstract could be reliably resolved through S2/OpenAlex/CORE/NDPR; automated
NDPR keyword-matching initially returned two clearly incorrect abstracts (for
the Popper and Giladi entries, matched to unrelated books via keyword overlap
on "poverty" and "Frankfurt School" respectively) which were manually removed
to avoid misattributing content.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize by reception rather than chronology: (1) Left/Right Hegelians and Marx's
inversion, (2) Popper's charge and its rebuttals, (3) Frankfurt School ambivalence,
(4) comparative teleologies (Kant, Herder) and the Foucauldian counter-model. The
Popper debate is the most heavily documented sub-debate and can anchor the
section; use Beiser and Wilkins as the strongest scholarly rebuttals.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Marxist inversion thesis: 2 items (Marx primary, Kojève) - Hegelian dialectic retained as method, idealist metaphysics discarded
- Popper's historicism charge and defenses of Hegel: 6 items (Popper x2, Beiser, Suchting, Wilkins, Hammersley, Eidlin) - contested reading of Hegel as proto-totalitarian prophet-historicist
- Frankfurt School ambivalent retrieval: 3 items (Finlayson, O'Connor, Giladi ed.) - critical theory's simultaneous dependence on and departure from Hegelian dialectic
- Comparative/counter teleologies: 5 items (Kant primary, James, Bristow, Foucault primary, Carr, Manley) - Kantian regulative and Herderian universal-history rivals; Foucauldian anti-teleological genealogy
- Young Hegelian reception: 1 item (Moggach ed.) - Feuerbach, Bauer, Strauss as the immediate political-philosophical context for Marx's turn
====================================================================
}

@book{hodgsonbrown2025lectures,
  author = {Hodgson, Peter C. and Brown, Robert F.},
  title = {Hegel: Lectures on the Philosophy of World History, Volume II},
  year = {2025},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/9780198943273.001.0001},
  abstract = {The first volume, containing the Manuscripts of the Introduction and the Lectures of 1822–23, was published by OUP in 2011. The second volume, containing the last of Hegel's lectures on this topic, had to await the German critical edition of the Lectures of 1830–31, which appeared in 2020. The main source is the transcription by Karl Hegel (the philosopher's son), but three other transcriptions of these lectures have been examined, and variations and additions from these other sources are included in an elaborate apparatus. A simplified and abbreviated version of the apparatus appears in translation. The manuscripts of the introduction contained in volume 1 include Hegel's own incomplete lecture manuscript for the Introduction to the Lectures in 1830–31. The transcription of the lectures as delivered provides an interesting comparison with what Hegel actually wrote. The present volume contains a lengthy Introduction that includes a discussion of the nature of Spirit, the concrete existence of freedom, the means Spirit uses, the state and ethical life, erroneous views of freedom and the state, constitution or systems of government, the course of world history, and the usefulness of world history. A preliminary part includes sections on climate and geography, the distinction between the old world and the new world, how the course of world history moves from East to West, and the four main divisions of it (Oriental, Greek, Roman, and European). While the German editors have provided complete critical editions of the lectures for every year that Hegel delivered them, only the first and last years appear in our English edition.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This is the second (and concluding) volume of the Oxford critical translation of Hegel's lecture courses on world history, covering the 1830-31 lectures. Its main source is Karl Hegel's transcription, checked against three other student transcripts, with variants recorded in an apparatus; this had to await the 2020 German critical edition of the 1830-31 course. The editors note that only the first (1822-23) and last (1830-31) of the years Hegel lectured on this material are translated in full into English, even though the German critical edition covers every year.

  RELEVANCE: Directly documents the manuscript/transcript problem the domain foregrounds: no single authorial text exists, only Hegel's incomplete introduction manuscript plus multiple, sometimes divergent, Nachschriften. Establishes that current English-language scholarship on the "late" Hegel's philosophy of history rests on a translation that is itself an edited selection from a larger German critical apparatus.

  POSITION: Represents the most recent stage of the editorial tradition, superseding composite 19th-century texts by anchoring translation to dated, individually attested lecture courses.
  },
  keywords = {textual-history, editions, lectures-on-world-history, High}
}

