  abstract = {Abstract The edition of the Corpus Hegelianum by disciples and friends of Hegel (1832 ff.) was an enormous achievement. It nevertheless shows grave shortcomings due to its instrumental usage in the disputes of that time. Thus since 1968 the Corpus is being replaced by the new edition "Hegel: Gesammelte Werke". This edition contains Hegel's published and posthumous works (vols. 1-22) as well as the transcripts of his lectures (vols. 23-30, in several fascicles). Taking Hegel's "Lecture on the Philosophy of Subjective Spirit" as an example, part II of this paper presents the transcripts as conveying an abundance of novel texts, which, furnished with explicative annotations, can give us new insights into the 'Berliner Hegel'.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jaeschke and Bauer explain why the original Corpus Hegelianum (the disciples' and friends' edition begun in 1832, from which the "Philosophy of History" volume derives) is being replaced, since 1968, by the Hegel-Archiv's Gesammelte Werke, which separates published/posthumous works (vols. 1-22) from lecture transcripts (vols. 23-30) and treats the transcripts as primary textual evidence rather than raw material for a composite paraphrase.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the institutional and methodological rationale for the entire post-1968 critical-edition tradition (Hegel-Archiv, Bochum) that underlies the Hodgson/Brown translations and the German edition of the 1830-31 world-history lectures used in Hodgson and Brown (2025). Explains concretely (via the Subjective Spirit lectures) how transcript-based editing changes what counts as Hegelian text.

  POSITION: Articulates the editorial-scholarly position (Hegel-Archiv/Gesammelte Werke) that the 19th-century Werke tradition instrumentalized Hegel's lecture material and that only systematic transcript editing can correct this.
